WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:07.860
You open a web browser and visit three pages: a search page, then a news site, then an article.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.518
Now you press the Back button.

00:00:10.518 --> 00:00:12.210
Which page do you get?

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.500
You're back on the news site.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:17.260
Press Back again, and you get the search page.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.640
The pages come back in the opposite order to the one you visited them in.

00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.625
Undo in a text editor works the same way.

00:00:24.625 --> 00:00:27.710
It always cancels your most recent change first.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:32.962
Both keep a pile of things, and only ever touch the one on top.

00:00:32.962 --> 00:00:35.140
That pile is called a stack.

00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:43.573
Welcome to Lecture 10 of Introduction to AI Programming: stacks, queues, linked lists and trees.

00:00:43.923 --> 00:00:48.442
In this first part, we'll define the stack, build one as a Python class,

00:00:48.442 --> 00:00:55.283
and use it to solve three problems: reversing a list, matching brackets, and checking the tags of a web page.

00:00:56.067 --> 00:00:58.987
First, two words we'll use all lecture.

00:00:59.337 --> 00:01:04.690
A data structure is a systematic way of organising and accessing data.

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:11.497
An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure that performs a task in a finite amount of time.

00:01:11.847 --> 00:01:16.135
Lecture 9 was mostly about algorithms: searching and sorting.

00:01:16.135 --> 00:01:18.807
This lecture is about data structures.

00:01:19.157 --> 00:01:23.843
You already know Python's built-in ones: list, tuple, dict and set.

00:01:23.843 --> 00:01:27.480
Today we build four more on top of them, as classes,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.837
each fast at some operations and slow at others.

00:01:31.634 --> 00:01:40.767
A stack is a collection of objects that are inserted and removed by one rule: last in, first out.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.274
For short, LIFO.

00:01:42.624 --> 00:01:44.528
Think of a stack of plates.

00:01:44.528 --> 00:01:47.984
You put a new plate on top. That's called a push.

00:01:48.334 --> 00:01:51.236
And you take a plate from the top. That's called a pop.

00:01:51.236 --> 00:01:53.294
You never pull one out from the middle.

00:01:53.644 --> 00:01:58.764
The plate you can see, the most recent one still there, is the top of the stack.

00:01:59.114 --> 00:02:01.009
You've met a stack before.

00:02:01.009 --> 00:02:05.895
In Lecture 6, every function call pushed a frame on the call stack,

00:02:05.895 --> 00:02:07.936
and every return popped one.

00:02:07.936 --> 00:02:11.874
That's why the last call made was the first to return.

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:15.467
A stack supports five operations.

00:02:15.817 --> 00:02:18.089
Push adds an element on top.

00:02:18.089 --> 00:02:21.577
Pop removes the top element and returns it.

00:02:21.927 --> 00:02:25.440
Top returns the top element without removing it.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.807
Both pop and top are errors on an empty stack.

00:02:29.157 --> 00:02:32.018
Is empty tells you whether there's anything there.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.136
And len gives the number of elements,

00:02:34.136 --> 00:02:36.997
which in a class we write as the method `__len__`.

00:02:37.347 --> 00:02:44.305
Notice that this list says what each operation does, but not how the elements are stored.

00:02:44.305 --> 00:02:49.309
A description like that is called an abstract data type, or ADT.

00:02:49.309 --> 00:02:52.827
Any class with these five methods is a stack.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.246
So how do we store them?

00:02:55.246 --> 00:02:59.223
A Python list already adds and removes at its end quickly.

00:02:59.223 --> 00:03:02.720
So let the end of the list be the top of the stack.

00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.563
The constructor makes an empty list called `_data`.

00:03:06.563 --> 00:03:11.150
The underscore says: this is internal, don't touch it from outside.

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.666
Push is just `append`.

00:03:13.666 --> 00:03:18.980
Watch the list: 100, then 200, then 300 go on the end.

00:03:19.330 --> 00:03:22.634
Top returns the last element, at index -1.

00:03:22.634 --> 00:03:28.770
Pop calls the list's own `pop`, which removes the last element and returns it.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.319
And if the stack is empty, top and pop raise an `IndexError`,

00:03:34.319 --> 00:03:36.960
instead of quietly carrying on.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:39.637
Two small changes from the slides.

00:03:39.637 --> 00:03:44.951
The slides write two underscores, and they print a message instead of raising an error.

00:03:44.951 --> 00:03:47.760
We'll see in a minute why the error is better.

00:03:48.534 --> 00:03:52.194
Here's the slides' program that uses the class.

00:03:52.194 --> 00:03:55.621
It makes a stack, prints whether it's empty,

00:03:55.621 --> 00:03:57.490
pushes 100, 200 and 300,

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:00.294
then prints top, pop, and top again.

00:04:00.644 --> 00:04:04.080
Pause the video and predict the four lines it prints.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.804
And how many elements are left at the end?

00:04:07.354 --> 00:04:10.035
First, the stack is empty: True.

00:04:10.035 --> 00:04:13.554
Then top looks at 300 without removing it.

00:04:13.904 --> 00:04:18.830
So pop returns the same 300, and this time it's gone.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:20.504
Now 200 is on top.

00:04:20.854 --> 00:04:25.054
Two elements are left: 100 and 200.

00:04:25.834 --> 00:04:28.147
What if you pop an empty stack?

00:04:28.147 --> 00:04:33.594
With our class, you find out at once: `IndexError`, pop from empty stack.

00:04:33.944 --> 00:04:39.235
With the slide version, the method prints a message, and then returns `None`.

00:04:39.235 --> 00:04:43.565
The program carries on, with a `None` where a number should be,

00:04:43.565 --> 00:04:46.864
and the bug shows up much later, somewhere else.

00:04:47.214 --> 00:04:51.254
So always check `is_empty` before you pop.

00:04:52.034 --> 00:04:53.509
Now let's use the stack.

00:04:53.509 --> 00:04:58.794
A stack is the right tool whenever the last thing you saw is the first thing you need.

00:04:59.144 --> 00:05:01.289
Problem 1: reverse a list.

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:04.424
Push every element, then pop them all.

00:05:04.774 --> 00:05:07.824
1, 2, 3, 4, 5 go in.

00:05:07.824 --> 00:05:09.654
5 is on top.

00:05:10.004 --> 00:05:14.810
So 5 comes out first, then 4, 3, 2, 1.

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:18.604
The new list is 5, 4, 3, 2, 1.

00:05:19.401 --> 00:05:21.532
Problem 2: matching brackets.

00:05:21.532 --> 00:05:28.513
In a correct expression, every opening bracket is closed by a closing bracket of the same kind,

00:05:28.513 --> 00:05:32.041
and they close in the reverse order they opened.

00:05:32.391 --> 00:05:39.096
The bracket that must close next is always the most recent one that's still open.

00:05:39.096 --> 00:05:41.911
Most recent first: that's a stack.

00:05:42.511 --> 00:05:43.796
So here's the rule.

00:05:43.796 --> 00:05:45.689
An opening bracket, push it.

00:05:45.689 --> 00:05:51.371
A closing bracket, pop the stack and check that the popped bracket is the same kind.

00:05:51.371 --> 00:05:56.511
And if the stack is empty, there's nothing to match, so the answer is False.

00:05:56.861 --> 00:06:02.141
At the very end, the expression is correct only if the stack is empty.

00:06:02.491 --> 00:06:05.251
Let's trace it on this one, which should fail.

00:06:05.601 --> 00:06:09.641
Three opening round brackets: push, push, push.

00:06:09.991 --> 00:06:12.810
A closing round bracket pops a round one.

00:06:12.810 --> 00:06:13.911
Same kind, good.

00:06:14.261 --> 00:06:16.402
Then a closing curly brace.

00:06:16.402 --> 00:06:18.305
It pops a round bracket.

00:06:18.305 --> 00:06:23.461
That's the wrong kind, so the function returns False right there.

00:06:23.811 --> 00:06:26.728
The line that checks the kind compares positions.

00:06:26.728 --> 00:06:30.122
The index of the closing bracket in the string of closers

00:06:30.122 --> 00:06:34.171
must equal the index of the popped bracket in the string of openers.

00:06:34.967 --> 00:06:38.603
Here's a tempting shortcut: just count the brackets.

00:06:38.603 --> 00:06:43.287
If there are as many opening ones as closing ones, it must be fine?

00:06:43.637 --> 00:06:43.950
No.

00:06:43.950 --> 00:06:49.475
These two both have equal counts, and both are wrong.

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:52.498
Counting can't see the order.

00:06:52.498 --> 00:06:53.957
The stack can.

00:06:54.734 --> 00:06:58.392
Problem 3: HTML, the language of web pages.

00:06:58.392 --> 00:07:00.689
It marks up text with tags.

00:07:00.689 --> 00:07:04.347
An opening tag is a name in angle brackets.

00:07:04.347 --> 00:07:06.814
The closing tag adds a slash.

00:07:07.164 --> 00:07:09.939
Tags must nest exactly like brackets,

00:07:09.939 --> 00:07:12.564
so the same stack idea checks them.

00:07:12.914 --> 00:07:18.291
The function finds each opening angle bracket, then the closing one after it,

00:07:18.291 --> 00:07:21.154
and takes the text in between as the tag.

00:07:21.504 --> 00:07:23.808
A tag without a slash is pushed.

00:07:23.808 --> 00:07:27.984
A tag with a slash must match the tag on top of the stack.

00:07:28.334 --> 00:07:29.546
Try this one.

00:07:29.546 --> 00:07:30.385
Push 'p'.

00:07:30.385 --> 00:07:31.224
Push 'b'.

00:07:31.224 --> 00:07:36.910
Now the closing 'p' arrives, but the top of the stack is 'b'.

00:07:36.910 --> 00:07:38.774
Wrong tag, so False.

00:07:39.124 --> 00:07:44.560
The slides' sample page, with its heading, paragraph and list, is correctly nested,

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.524
and the function returns True.

00:07:47.301 --> 00:07:48.501
Let's sum up.

00:07:48.851 --> 00:07:51.681
A stack is last in, first out.

00:07:51.681 --> 00:07:53.851
You only touch the top.

00:07:54.201 --> 00:07:59.099
Its operations are push, pop, top, is empty and len,

00:07:59.099 --> 00:08:04.561
and a Python list stores it well, with the top at the end.

00:08:04.911 --> 00:08:10.034
Use a stack whenever the most recent item must be dealt with first:

00:08:10.034 --> 00:08:13.551
reversing, brackets, tags, and the call stack.

00:08:13.901 --> 00:08:15.536
Here's one to try.

00:08:15.536 --> 00:08:20.258
Write `is_palindrome`, using a stack and no slicing.

00:08:20.258 --> 00:08:27.341
Push every character, then pop them and compare with the string read forwards.

00:08:27.891 --> 00:08:31.654
In part two, we'll meet the stack's opposite, the queue,

00:08:31.654 --> 00:08:34.611
where the first one in is the first one out.

